// Package ir declares the compiler's own vocabulary: the constructs a
// declaration can name that stand for something in the compiler rather than
// for a value in the program.
//
// `Macro` is the centre of it. A function whose return type is `Macro` is a
// macro — a name that may be written as a `#[...]` mark and nowhere else. The
// declaration is not what runs; a Go handler registered under the same package
// and name transforms the declaration the mark annotates. It exists so that a
// macro has somewhere to carry its doc comment and its argument list, which is
// what `sngl doc` reads.
//
// The flag enums of marks declared elsewhere live here too, rather than beside
// the mark. A mark's package is dot-imported — that is how a bare `#[builtin]`
// resolves — and a dot import of a lib package lifts what it declares, so an
// enum next to the mark would end up in the scope of every program that
// imports the package that imports it. Nothing dot-imports this one.

// The result type that makes a function a macro.
//
// It has no values and no representation: nothing constructs one, no field or
// variable can hold one, and the checker registers no function for a
// declaration that returns one — a macro is written as a mark, never called.
struct Macro {}

// What the compiler may assume about a call to an intrinsic. Written as bare
// identifiers after the id: `#[intrinsic("list.push", mutates, mutatesReceiver)]`.
//
// What the declaration's SNGL body is good for is not among them. A body says
// it: one that is written computes the same result the native implementation
// would, so a backend that does not implement the id may emit it, and a
// declaration with no body is a signature only. That used to be a `usable` flag
// beside the body — two records of one fact, and the flag was one word away from
// promoting a fabricated `return 0` into a live wrong answer.
//
// `mutates` says a call has an effect beyond its result, so it survives
// dead-code elimination and is never folded. `readonly` says the result depends
// on host state — the clock, the environment, the filesystem — so it must not be
// folded either, though the call has no effect of its own; it contradicts
// `mutates`. `mutatesReceiver` says the first argument is modified in place,
// which is what reactivity needs to treat a statement-level call as a write to
// the receiver's variable.
enum IntrinsicFlag { mutates, readonly, mutatesReceiver }

// What a call to a foreign function costs. Written as bare identifiers after
// the name: `#[foreign("go:example.com/api", "Get", pure, async)]`.
//
// `pure` says a call has no effects and no dependence on host state, so the
// compiler may evaluate one at build time — the sharp edge of the mark, since
// a function wrongly marked pure runs during a build. `async` says the
// declaration returns a promise its caller awaits, the fact the TypeScript
// importer reads off `Promise<T>`.
//
// Both describe a call, so a declaration with no call to describe — a struct,
// a field — refuses them.
enum ForeignFlag { pure, async }
